><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20251014095248+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20251014095250+10'00'" name="1472b329-8721-44ad-807e-9d85690d6e3d" page="2" coords="49.548920,260.532898,288.644836,260.532898,49.548920,245.772507,288.644836,245.772507,37.587387,250.045059,288.656738,250.045059,37.587387,235.284653,288.656738,235.284622,37.587402,239.612961,288.663940,239.612961,37.587402,224.852554,288.663940,224.852554,37.587402,229.125107,288.691162,229.125107,37.587402,214.364700,288.691162,214.364700,37.587418,218.693832,288.680786,218.693832,37.587418,203.933426,288.680786,203.933426,37.587433,208.205978,288.672638,208.205978,37.587433,193.445572,288.672638,193.445572,37.587418,197.773911,288.645538,197.773911,37.587418,183.013504,288.645538,183.013504,37.587402,187.285995,288.668823,187.285995,37.587402,172.525589,288.668823,172.525589,37.587402,176.854782,288.642395,176.854782,37.587402,162.094376,288.642395,162.094376,37.587402,166.366867,288.688660,166.366928,37.587402,151.606461,288.688660,151.606461,37.587402,155.935654,288.683716,155.935654,37.587402,141.175247,288.683716,141.175247,37.587402,145.447006,267.927368,145.447006,37.587402,130.686600,267.927368,130.686600" rect="37.126114,130.225327,289.152435,260.994171" subject="Comment on Text" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>The examples provided in this editorial raise awareness of the scope for mental fatigue and mental recovery to influence a range of occupational domains, where large potential exists for improvement in management of the phenomena. Despite the progress made to date, there remains a need for more collaborative and translational research centred around applied and representative investigations. Such research can be used to inform best-practice to mitigate both the acute and potentially chronic consequences of mental fatigue. Capitalising on the current growth in technological advancement, there is a continued need for the development, evaluation, and subsequent integration of sensitive, reliable, and robust behavioural and neuro-physiological assessments capable of being implemented in real-time, field-based scenarios.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="2" rect="595.276001,168.532898,779.276001,260.532898"
/></highlight
